<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf Coast of Mexico</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf Coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> is the lowland region along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, covering modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>. It was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the Olmec, the first complex society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, and later of El Tajín and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totonacs">Totonacs</a>. Peoples and traders from the Gulf lowlands, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmeca-xicalanca">Olmeca-Xicalanca</a>, carried influences into the central highlands, Yucatán and the Maya world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico.jpg" alt="Gulf Coast of Mexico" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulf_of_Mexico_ETOPO_2022_(labeled).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
